---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import CardAnalysis from "@components/CardAnalysis.astro";
import { Card } from "webcoreui/astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
---

<Layout title="Playing Through Hate - Replenish Guide">
    <PageHeader
        title="Playing Through Hate"
        description="Mastering post-sideboard matches requires understanding how to navigate around targeted opposing interaction. You do not need an explicit answer to every sideboard threat; rather, winning Games 2 and 3 relies on altering your sequencing, timing your stack triggers, and executing disciplined counterplay."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/mage.webp"
        imageAlt="Meddling Mage"
        imageCaption="Meddling Mage by Christopher Moeller"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="Graveyard Interference" titleTag="h2">
        <p class="section-intro">
            Because <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                >Replenish</a
            > relies heavily on the graveyard to cheat massive mana costs, opponents
            will inevitably present graveyard disruption in post-board games. The
            secret to defeating graveyard hate is simple: never commit critical resources
            blindly without an immediate win condition on the stack or in hand.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Withered Wretch"
            image="creatures/withered_wretch.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>(Black Aggro, The Rock):</strong>
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Withered Wretch"
                    >Withered Wretch</a
                > is exceptionally dangerous because its graveyard exile activation
                is repeatable, instant-speed, and attached to an aggressive creature
                body that clocks your life total.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> When playing against black midrange
                or aggro, strictly preserve your copies of <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                    >Swords to Plowshares</a
                > specifically for Withered Wretch. Do not waste interaction on redundant
                aggressive beaters like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Jackal Pup">Jackal Pup</a
                > or <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Llanowar Elves">Llanowar Elves</a
                > if doing so leaves you helpless against a Wretch. Prioritize removing
                it before activating card selection engines or committing enchantments
                to your graveyard.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Tormod's Crypt"
            image="artifacts/tormods_crypt.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>Universal Exile:</strong> The simplest physical answer to
                an early <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                > is destroying it with <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Disenchant"
                    >Disenchant</a
                > before initiating a combo loop. However, relying purely on finding
                removal is undisciplined; knowing how to out-sequence Crypt on the
                stack is a foundational Replenish skill.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>The Stack Trick:</strong> Against an active Crypt on the battlefield,
                do not blindly dump your entire hand into your graveyard. Instead,
                maintain an active <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                > on the table while retaining your winning combo pieces in hand.
                With a moderate, tempting graveyard (just enough value to bait activation),
                cast <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                    >Replenish</a
                >. When your opponent sacrifices Crypt in response to purge your
                existing yard, Replenish remains sitting on the stack. Still in
                response to your own Replenish, activate your Attunement to dump
                a fresh hand of combo enchantments into your newly cleared
                graveyard. Replenish resolves instantly thereafter, returning
                everything you just discarded.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Phyrexian Furnace"
            image="artifacts/phyrexian_furnace.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>Incremental Attrition:</strong> Unlike <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Phyrexian Furnace">Phyrexian Furnace</a
                > exiles only one card at a time via targeted sacrifice or random
                tap activations.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Never commit your combo enchantments
                to the graveyard gradually over several turns—this simply invites
                your opponent to snipe key lock pieces one by one. Instead, execute
                your setup explosively in a single turn via <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Intuition"
                    >Intuition</a
                > immediately before casting <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                >. By overwhelming their single targeted exile activation on the
                stack, they can capture at most one card while the rest of your
                architecture returns to end the game.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Haunting Echoes"
            image="sorceries/haunting_echoes.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>Graveyard & Library Extraction:</strong> The supreme terminal
                hate piece of Black-based control shells and Psychatog variants. Resolving
                a five-mana <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Haunting Echoes">Haunting Echoes</a
                > obliterates your accumulated graveyard while scouring your entire
                library of all namesake copies, completely disabling your combo architecture.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Because Haunting Echoes is a heavy five-mana
                sorcery, opponents are forced to tap down significantly during their
                own main phase to deploy it. Preserve cheap blue countermagic (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Mana Leak">Mana Leak</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Counterspell"
                    >Counterspell</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Arcane Denial"
                    >Arcane Denial</a
                >) specifically for their fifth mana threshold, or proactively
                lock out the spell via <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                > in Games 2 and 3. Avoid passively building an overflowing graveyard
                against five-mana black control pools without an immediate plan to
                execute Replenish on your own turn.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Planar Void" image="enchantments/planar_void.jpg">
            <p>
                <strong>Continuous Exile Constraint:</strong> A one-mana static enchantment
                favored by Suicide Black and aggressive mono-black builds that automatically
                exiles any card immediately as it hits the graveyard, creating an
                oppressive graveyard vacuum.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay & Trigger Manipulation:</strong> Because <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Planar Void">Planar Void</a
                > is an enchantment itself, our maindeck copies of <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > or instant-speed <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
                > cleanly destroy it before attempting combo loops. Furthermore, for
                advanced stack mastery: Planar Void operates via a triggered ability
                ("whenever another card is put into a graveyard, exile it") rather
                than an instantaneous replacement effect. When an enchantment enters
                your graveyard, Void's exile trigger is placed on the stack; if you
                can initiate instant-speed recursion or respond before the trigger
                resolves, you can exploit brief tactical windows, though prior physical
                removal remains the gold standard.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Sweepers & Permanent Wipes" titleTag="h2">
        <p class="section-intro">
            When opponents fail to attack your graveyard, they frequently
            overload on reactive anti-enchantment removal to dismantle your
            engines once they reach the battlefield.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Tranquil Domain / Naturalize"
            image="instants/tranquil_domain.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>Instant-Speed Destruction:</strong> Traditional green answers
                aimed at destroying your lock pieces as they are cast or attacking
                your established board during combat or end steps.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong>
                <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                > serves as the premier overarching defense: returning Decree alongside
                your combo forces the opponent to exhaust three spells just to break
                through the automatic counter shielding. Furthermore, under an active
                Opal-Wave engine, you can activate <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                > targeting your own vulnerable enchantments in response to removal;
                once the stack clears and Wave triggers, your board returns unharmed
                and refreshed. Finally, transitioning into our post-sideboard creature
                package (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                >) completely invalidates opposing naturalize suites by
                presenting lethal damage from non-enchantment sources.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Scour" image="instants/scour.jpg">
            <p>
                <strong>Surgical Eradication:</strong> Primarily fielded by blue-white
                control sideboards utilizing Invasion-block removal suites, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Scour">Scour</a
                > threatens to destroy an enchantment on the battlefield and permanently
                exile all corresponding copies across your hand, library, and graveyard.
                A single resolved Scour targeting <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                > or <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                > can dismantle your foundational win condition for the rest of the
                match.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Never deploy an unprotected engine enchantment
                against open four-mana white suites post-sideboard without permission
                or a defensive plan. Most importantly, when initiating an Opal-Wave
                loop, maintain active fading counters on your Parallax Wave so you
                can instantly exile your own enchantments in response to Scour targeting
                them on the stack. If the target leaves the battlefield before resolution,
                Scour completely fizzles and zero copies are exiled.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Anarchy" image="sorceries/anarchy.jpg">
            <p>
                <strong>Red Sweeper & Color Hate:</strong> Aggressive red architectures
                (Sligh, RG Ponza, Goblins) consistently side in <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Anarchy">Anarchy</a
                > to destroy white stabilization engines like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Sphere of Law">Sphere of Law</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Exalted Angel"
                    >Exalted Angel</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage"
                    >Meddling Mage</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence"
                    >Opalescence</a
                >, and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                > in a single sweeping blow.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Anarchy is a four-mana sorcery that
                destroys rather than exiles. First, proactively throttle their mana
                development via <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
                > and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Rishadan Port">Rishadan Port</a
                > to keep them off four operational mana, or name it defensively with
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage"
                    >Meddling Mage</a
                >. Second, recognize that because Anarchy sends all destroyed
                enchantments directly to your graveyard, retaining a backup copy
                of <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Replenish"
                    >Replenish</a
                > allows you to instantly reanimate your entire wiped board on your
                subsequent turn, turning an apparent catastrophe into immediate game-ending
                tempo.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Reverent Silence"
            image="sorceries/reverent_silence.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>Free Enchantment Sweeper:</strong> Utilized by Elves and green
                aggro-midrange builds as zero-mana interaction by having you gain
                6 life as an alternate casting cost. While sweeping your entire enchantment
                board for zero mana can ruin early stabilization pieces like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Sphere of Law">Sphere of Law</a
                > or <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement"
                    >Attunement</a
                >, the dynamic is deeply advantageous if planned correctly.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> As a sorcery, opponents are restricted
                to casting <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Reverent Silence">Reverent Silence</a
                > during their main phase. During preliminary setup rounds, gladly
                accept the 6-life cushion; every destroyed enchantment lands directly
                in your graveyard, actively advancing your <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > setup at zero card cost to you. Once your Opal-Wave engine is fully
                assembled, simply activate <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                > in response to stack self-protection triggers, bringing your entire
                board back safely once Reverent Silence resolves.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Ray of Revelation / Sylvan Reclamation">
            <p>
                <strong>Flashback & Exile Spot Removal:</strong> Green-white disruptive
                configurations rely on <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Ray of Revelation">Ray of Revelation</a
                > for devastating 2-for-1 enchantment attrition via Flashback, while
                extended format analyses frequently emphasize permanent exile sweepers
                like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Sylvan Reclamation">Sylvan Reclamation</a
                > to deny graveyard recursion entirely. Countering the front half
                of Ray from hand fails to prevent an opponent from paying one white
                mana to destroy your key piece directly from the graveyard.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Against recurring flashback interaction,
                do not rely on sequential, piece-by-piece enchantment deployments.
                Execute simultaneous multi-enchantment installations via <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > where an instant-speed <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                > can protect your lock pieces, or shift tactical focus entirely toward
                transformational non-enchantment win conditions (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Meddling Mage"
                    >Meddling Mage</a
                >) that leave opposing enchantment removal completely dead in
                hand.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Calming Verse">
            <p>
                <strong>Asymmetric Mass Sweeper:</strong> A catastrophic four-mana
                green sorcery sweeper that destroys all enchantments you control while
                leaving a tapped-out or green opponent's permanent infrastructure
                completely unharmed.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Calming Verse demands rigorous permission
                discipline: preserve hard blue countermagic (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Mana Leak">Mana Leak</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Counterspell"
                    >Counterspell</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Arcane Denial"
                    >Arcane Denial</a
                >) strictly for their four-mana sorcery deployment window.
                Alternatively, deploy proactive interaction like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Orim's Chant"
                    >Orim's Chant</a
                >, or <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Defense Grid">Defense Grid</a
                > to strip their ability to safely clear the table during decisive
                setup turns.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Taxation & Cost Increases" titleTag="h2">
        <p class="section-intro">
            Static restriction permanents attempt to lock down your resource
            development or prevent your core engine loops from executing
            cleanly.
        </p>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Presence of the Master"
            image="enchantments/presence_of_the_master.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>The Replenish Bypass:</strong> While imposing on paper against
                an enchantment archetype, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Presence of the Master"
                    >Presence of the Master</a
                > strictly counters enchantment spells that are <em>played</em> (cast
                from your hand). <strong
                    >It does not intercept enchantments entering the battlefield
                    via <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                    >!</strong
                >
            </p>
            <p>
                However, a resolved Presence prevents you from casting manual
                combo pieces or hardcasting answers like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                >. To navigate around it, focus entirely on sculpting a decisive
                graveyard and winning directly off a single Replenish, or rely
                on instant-speed removal like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
                > and non-enchantment proactive threats like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                > and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                >.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Aura of Silence"
            image="enchantments/aura_of_silence.jpg"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>Tax & Threat Combination:</strong> Frequently sideboarded
                in White Weenie, BW Control, and Enchantress mirrors. Because <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Aura of Silence">Aura of Silence</a
                > increases the casting cost of your artifacts and enchantments by
                two generic mana, deploying early ramp or hardcasting combo engines
                becomes agonizingly slow. Worse still, sitting openly on the battlefield,
                it represents an immediate sacrifice trigger to destroy whatever key
                enchantment you do manage to resolve.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> This interaction underscores precisely
                why our sideboard engineers a split between <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > and <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Disenchant"
                    >Disenchant</a
                >. Disenchant remains a clean two-mana instant entirely immune
                to Aura's static tax. In attrition matchups where Aura hits the
                table early, concentrate your mana on resolving <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                > and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > to load the graveyard, then cast a multi-enchantment <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                >; a single sacrifice trigger cannot dismantle a complete
                Opal-Wave-Decree lock entering simultaneously.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Gloom" image="matchups/moneyball/gloom.jpg">
            <p>
                <strong>Asymmetric Color Tax:</strong> Brought in by Black aggro,
                Suicide Black, and The Rock. By taxing all white spells and white
                enchantment activation costs by three generic mana, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Gloom">Gloom</a
                > threatens to cripple <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                    >Swords to Plowshares</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence"
                    >Opalescence</a
                >, and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                >.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Fortunately, Gloom is strictly color-scoped
                to white. Your entire blue card-selection engine (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Intuition"
                    >Intuition</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Deep Analysis"
                    >Deep Analysis</a
                >) and blue permission suite (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Mana Leak">Mana Leak</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Counterspell"
                    >Counterspell</a
                >) function entirely unimpeded. Against heavily black fields,
                reserve early blue countermagic strictly for intercepting Gloom
                on the stack. If resolved, leverage sol lands (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Ancient Tomb">Ancient Tomb</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="City of Traitors">City of Traitors</a
                >) and artifact ramp to manually overcome the three-mana
                surcharge over extended games, or rely on blue bounce mechanics
                like <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Upheaval"
                    >Upheaval</a
                > and cycling <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                >.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Winter Orb" image="artifacts/winter_orb.jpg">
            <p>
                <strong>Mana Chokepoints:</strong> Featured heavily in Stasis, Merfolk,
                and aggressive tempo builds. Because Replenish operates around a critical
                three- and four-mana spell curve, restricted untap steps severely
                constrain your operational bandwidth and setup speed.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Prioritize removing <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Winter Orb">Winter Orb</a
                > immediately with <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > or instant-speed <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
                >. In game lines where Orb persists, sol lands like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Ancient Tomb">Ancient Tomb</a
                > provide immense tactical leverage by generating two mana off a single
                untap step. Most importantly, remember that <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                > functions as an extraordinary burst untapper under Winter Orb: tap
                all available lands for mana before casting Search, then untap three
                lands upon resolution to suddenly generate a massive mana pool during
                a choked turn.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Cursed Totem" image="artifacts/cursed_totem.jpg">
            <p>
                <strong>The Opalescence Lockout:</strong> A subtle and deceptively
                dangerous sideboard artifact. On an empty board or during standard
                setup phases, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Cursed Totem">Cursed Totem</a
                > has zero interaction with normal enchantments. However, <strong
                    >the instant <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                    > enters the battlefield, all of your non-Aura enchantments transform
                    into creatures.</strong
                >
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Under Opalescence and Cursed Totem simultaneously,
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave"
                    >Parallax Wave</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Attunement"
                    >Attunement</a
                >, and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > are strictly considered creatures with activated abilities—meaning
                Cursed Totem completely disables their activation loops! To circumvent
                this critical gotcha, always utilize <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > or instant-speed <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
                > to destroy Cursed Totem <em>before</em> deploying Opalescence onto
                the battlefield.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Dystopia" image="enchantments/dystopia.jpg">
            <p>
                <strong>Cumulative Color Attrition:</strong> A grueling sideboard
                enchantment deployed by Black control and midrange configurations
                (such as The Rock) that imposes a recurring upkeep tax: at the beginning
                of your upkeep, you are forced to sacrifice a green or white permanent.
                Left unchecked, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Dystopia">Dystopia</a
                > aggressively strips away your <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Parallax Wave"
                    >Parallax Wave</a
                >, and <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Sphere of Law">Sphere of Law</a
                > structures.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Crucially, Dystopia demands progressive
                cumulative upkeep life payments from the opponent and is entirely
                color-restricted to green and white—meaning your foundational blue
                draw-discard engine (<a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
                >, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
                >) and sol artifact base operate completely immune to its
                effect. Simply accumulate your combo payload safely inside your
                graveyard without casting premature white permanents; when
                ready, deploy <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > onto an overwhelming board where sacrificing a single redundant
                white permanent to Dystopia's upkeep trigger is irrelevant before
                executing lethal attacks.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis title="Humility">
            <p>
                <strong>Layered Stat Normalization:</strong> Occasional mirror tech
                or UW Control sideboard inclusion, <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Humility">Humility</a
                > strips all creature abilities and forces base stats to 1/1. Because
                our lethal endgame relies on <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                > transforming our non-Aura enchantments into dynamic attacking creatures,
                a resolved Humility creates complex layer conflicts and neutralizes
                your creature-based engine activations if timestamped over your setup.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> When Humility is fielded, do not attempt
                to win through combat damage with Opalescence creatures or rely on
                creature-state activated abilities. Treat Humility as a mandatory
                removal target: systematically clear it from the table via instant-speed
                <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Disenchant"
                    >Disenchant</a
                > or an existing <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                > prior to transitioning your graveyard architecture into active battlefield
                combat.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Proactive Creature Interference" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Meddling Mage"
            image="creatures/meddling_mage.webp"
        >
            <p>
                <strong>Precision Bottlenecks:</strong> Opponents fielding blue-white
                builds (UW Landstill, mirrors) frequently introduce <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                > post-board to lock down your namesake <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                > or foundational combo engines like <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                >.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>Counterplay:</strong> Maintain vigilant situational awareness
                regarding which opponent archetypes have access to white and blue
                mana after Games 1. When anticipating opposing Mages, deliberately
                retain spot removal suite representation (leaving in 1–2 copies of
                <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
                    >Swords to Plowshares</a
                > or relying on <a
                    href="#"
                    class="card-hover"
                    data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                >). A single Meddling Mage represents a temporary hurdle rather
                than a hard lockout if you construct your sideboard transitions
                to expect it.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>
